        "section": [
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">COPD exacerbation, treated with bronchodilators and steroids</div>"
            },
            "code": {
              "coding": [
                {
                  "code": "67781-5",
                  "system": "http://loinc.org",
                  "display": "Summarization of encounter note"
                }
              ]
            },
            "title": "Synthesis"
          },
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">Dear colleague, I am writing to inform you about the recent hospital admission of your 84-year-old male patient, who presented with an acute exacerbation of his known chronic obstructive pulmonary disease. On admission, he exhibited increased dyspnoea, productive cough, and reduced oxygen saturation. Diagnostic workup included arterial blood gas analysis confirming respiratory insufficiency, chest X-ray showing hyperinflation without signs of pneumonia, and pulse oximetry monitoring. Blood tests revealed mild systemic inflammation. The patient was treated with inhaled bronchodilators via nebuliser, systemic corticosteroids, and supplemental oxygen therapy, leading to satisfactory clinical improvement. Vital signs were monitored continuously throughout the stay. The patient was discharged in stable condition with adjusted inhaler regimen and oral steroid taper. I recommend close follow-up of his pulmonary function and review of long-term COPD management. Please do not hesitate to contact me for further information.</div>"
            },
